WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.850
David: Welcome to LitShare.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.225
This is Protein Degradation English
Corner.

3
00:00:05.288 --> 00:00:06.538
Episode three.

4
00:00:07.325 --> 00:00:10.775
Cell Chemical Biology, Christina M. Woo.

5
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.100
The Degron, Rebuilt as a Warhead.

6
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.225
Here is the question.

7
00:00:18.275 --> 00:00:25.975
Last time, we learned that cereblon reads
a natural tag: a cyclic imide that forms
on ageing proteins.

8
00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:32.125
Now the question is, can you take that tag
and rebuild it as a drug?

9
00:00:33.025 --> 00:00:35.575
Can a degron become a warhead?

10
00:00:36.475 --> 00:00:40.000
Alex: That is what Christina Woo's team at
Harvard set out to do.

11
00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:42.325
Their paper is in Cell Chemical Biology,
2024.

12
00:00:45.025 --> 00:00:48.425
First authors Saki Ichikawa and Connor
Payne.

13
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.100
They built a new class of cereblon ligands
called the cyclimids.

14
00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:54.950
And they tested sixty of them.

15
00:00:55.525 --> 00:00:56.650
David: Start with the name.

16
00:00:57.675 --> 00:00:58.250
What is a cyclimid?

17
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:05.150
Alex: A cyclimid is a short dipeptide that
ends in a cyclic imide ring.

18
00:01:05.925 --> 00:01:13.975
Take the natural degron, attach it to a
molecule that binds your target protein,
and you have a bifunctional degrader.

19
00:01:14.425 --> 00:01:20.600
A PROTAC, a proteolysis targeting chimera,
with a degron for a warhead.

20
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.450
David: And they built a library?

21
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:23.625
Alex: Yes.

22
00:01:24.125 --> 00:01:29.650
Forty degraders against BRD4, a protein
that reads acetylated histones.

23
00:01:30.375 --> 00:01:34.025
Twenty with a six-membered ring, called
cQ, from glutamine.

24
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.050
Twenty with a five-membered ring, called
cN, from asparagine.

25
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.200
At the position next to the ring, they
installed all twenty amino acids, one by
one.

26
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.500
Same linker throughout, so the only
variable is the warhead.

27
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.350
David: What did they measure first?

28
00:01:52.225 --> 00:01:52.825
Alex: Binding to cereblon.

29
00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:03.150
They used TR-FRET, time-resolved
fluorescence energy transfer, a
competition assay with a fluorescent
thalidomide tracer.

30
00:02:04.825 --> 00:02:11.275
All forty degraders bound cereblon, with
dissociation constants, KDs, from eight
nanomolar to one micromolar.

31
00:02:13.525 --> 00:02:14.575
David: And the surprise?

32
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:16.500
Alex: The five-membered ring won.

33
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:23.275
The cN degraders generally bound cereblon
more tightly than their cQ counterparts.

34
00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:26.525
That is the opposite of thalidomide.

35
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.275
In thalidomide derivatives, shrinking the
ring to five members kills binding.

36
00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:39.025
Pomalidomide binds about five hundred
times more tightly than its five-membered
analog.

37
00:02:39.625 --> 00:02:42.175
David: Did that difference show up in
cells?

38
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:43.900
Alex: Dramatically.

39
00:02:44.675 --> 00:02:51.800
JQ1-FcN, the five-membered degrader,
cleared BRD4 at one hundred nanomolar.

40
00:02:52.525 --> 00:03:00.875
But dBET6-cN, the same five-membered ring
in a thalidomide scaffold, did nothing,
even at ten micromolar.

41
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:06.425
Same ring, different scaffold, opposite
result.

42
00:03:07.375 --> 00:03:08.925
David: So binding is one thing.

43
00:03:09.625 --> 00:03:15.350
What about the ternary complex, the three-
way assembly of degrader, target, and
ligase?

44
00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.700
Alex: That is the heart of the paper.

45
00:03:18.175 --> 00:03:23.425
They built a quantitative TR-FRET assay to
measure the ternary complex directly.

46
00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:09.900
They saturated the target, BRD4's
bromodomain, and measured how tightly the
degrader-target pair binds cereblon.

47
00:05:10.625 --> 00:05:12.375
That gives a ternary KD.

48
00:06:46.775 --> 00:06:55.725
And they calculated a cooperativity
factor, Alpha: the binary KD divided by
the ternary KD.

49
00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:03.750
Positive cooperativity means the two
proteins like each other once the degrader
brings them together.

50
00:07:04.475 --> 00:07:07.750
David: And the amino acid next to the ring
changed that?

51
00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:09.200
Alex: Completely.

52
00:07:09.775 --> 00:07:18.800
Same linker, same target, same ring, but
swapping one amino acid changed the
cooperativity eighty-fold between two
degraders.

53
00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:23.600
And the degraders showed a preference for
one bromodomain over the other.

54
00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:33.725
JQ1-YcQ was fifty-seven-fold selective for
forming a ternary complex with BD1 over
BD2.

55
00:07:34.175 --> 00:07:35.650
David: Why does that matter?

56
00:07:36.275 --> 00:07:44.050
Alex: Because BRD4 has two bromodomains,
BD1 and BD2, and most degraders hit both.

57
00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:52.500
If the warhead alone can pick one domain
over the other, you get selectivity
without redesigning the whole molecule.

58
00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:57.925
David: The big claim is that the ternary
complex measurement predicts degradation.

59
00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:00.150
How strong is the evidence?

60
00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:01.850
Alex: Very strong.

61
00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:12.025
They measured degradation of endogenous
BRD4 in cells for twenty degraders, and
plotted it against the ternary KD.

62
00:08:12.675 --> 00:08:18.025
The correlation coefficient was zero point
eight two, with a p-value below zero point
zero zero zero one.

63
00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:30.675
The degraders spanned a five-hundred-fold
range of DC50 values, the concentration
for half-maximal degradation.

64
00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:34.125
And the ternary KD predicted it.

65
00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:37.525
David: What about the domain selectivity?

66
00:08:38.125 --> 00:08:39.325
Did it hold up in cells?

67
00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:41.050
Alex: Yes.

68
00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:57.375
In reporter cells, JQ1-YcQ degraded BD1
with a DC50 of about one hundred
nanomolar, and BD2 at about five hundred
nanomolar, roughly a five-fold difference.

69
00:08:57.875 --> 00:09:09.050
The non-selective pair, JQ1-AcQ and
JQ1-AcN, hit both domains equally, just as
the biochemistry predicted.

70
00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:13.250
David: They also looked at kinetics, how
long the complex lasts.

71
00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:14.525
Alex: Right.

72
00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:23.450
The BD1 ternary complexes lasted from
under thirty seconds to two hundred
seventy seconds, depending on the
degrader.

73
00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:59.450
The most cooperative ones, JQ1-YcQ and
JQ1-YcN, lasted longest.

74
00:11:00.425 --> 00:11:04.900
The BD2 complexes all fell apart within
thirty seconds.

75
00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:17.425
And in cells, JQ1-YcQ reached maximum
degradation in one and a half hours for
BD1, but three hours for BD2.

76
00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:24.000
How long the complex holds together
matters, not just how tightly it forms.

77
00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:27.075
David: So far it is all BRD4.

78
00:11:27.725 --> 00:11:29.800
Does the platform work on other targets?

79
00:11:30.525 --> 00:11:31.800
Alex: That is the next test.

80
00:11:32.425 --> 00:11:40.100
They built ten degraders for FKBP12, and
ten for the kinases CDK4 and CDK6.

81
00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:43.300
Same warhead screen, different target
binder.

82
00:11:43.875 --> 00:11:51.125
For FKBP12, the ternary KD again predicted
degradation, with a correlation of zero
point eight one.

83
00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:55.975
And the degraders spared two close
relatives, FKBP fifty-one and FKBP fifty-
two.

84
00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:03.950
David: And what about the CDK degraders?

85
00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:11.300
Alex: All ten wiped out CDK6 at one
hundred nanomolar, and several worked at
ten nanomolar.

86
00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:15.475
But one stood out: dCDK-PcQ.

87
00:12:15.825 --> 00:12:29.725
It degraded CDK6 with a DC50 of two to
nine nanomolar, but barely touched CDK4,
with a DC50 of eighty-nine nanomolar and a
maximum degradation of only twenty-five
percent.

88
00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:36.700
A CDK6-selective degrader, from a single
amino acid choice.

89
00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:39.975
David: There was also something about the
hook effect.

90
00:12:39.975 --> 00:12:41.025
Alex: Yes.

91
00:12:41.475 --> 00:12:56.350
At high doses, ten micromolar, the six-
membered degraders and the pomalidomide
control showed the hook effect:
degradation drops because the degrader
saturates each protein separately instead
of bridging them.

92
00:12:57.625 --> 00:13:00.425
The five-membered cN degraders did not
show it.

93
00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:03.725
Another reason to screen both ring sizes.

94
00:13:05.175 --> 00:13:10.675
David: Thalidomide warheads have a known
problem: they drag in unwanted proteins,
the neosubstrates.

95
00:13:12.975 --> 00:13:13.575
Do the cyclimids?

96
00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:16.925
Alex: Almost not at all.

97
00:13:17.475 --> 00:13:29.850
They tested the classic thalidomide off-
targets, the zinc-finger proteins IKZF3
and ZFP91, and the translation factor
GSPT1.

98
00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:35.675
The thalidomide-based degrader ARV-825
wiped them out.

99
00:13:36.325 --> 00:13:42.000
The cyclimids barely moved them, with
maximum degradation of three percent or
less.

100
00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:50.850
Global proteomics confirmed it: dCDK-PcQ
depleted CDK6 alone.

101
00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:53.200
David: What about drug-like properties?

102
00:13:54.175 --> 00:13:55.400
These are peptides.

103
00:13:56.850 --> 00:14:05.225
Alex: The cyclimid degraders had similar
metabolic stability and cell permeability
to the thalidomide-based dBET6.

104
00:14:05.625 --> 00:14:09.950
And the standalone cyclimids were more
stable than thalidomide derivatives.

105
00:14:10.575 --> 00:14:17.175
They also reduce two classic thalidomide
problems: the stereocenter that flips, and
the ring that hydrolyses.

106
00:14:17.975 --> 00:14:22.075
David: What is the big idea here, beyond
this one paper?

107
00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:25.025
Alex: A new screening concept.

108
00:14:25.625 --> 00:14:31.600
Degrader discovery usually optimizes the
linker, a strategy called linkerology.

109
00:14:32.125 --> 00:14:35.500
This paper says: screen the warhead
instead.

110
00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:41.900
Swap one amino acid, and you sample a
whole new set of ternary complexes.

111
00:14:42.725 --> 00:14:48.475
It is orthogonal, and it is fast, because
the cyclimids are easy to make.

112
00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:50.125
David: What is still missing?

113
00:14:50.125 --> 00:14:51.500
Alex: Three things.

114
00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:55.600
First, everything here is biochemical and
cellular.

115
00:14:56.350 --> 00:14:57.950
There is no animal data yet.

116
00:14:58.750 --> 00:15:03.750
Second, the cyclimids are peptidic, so
their drug properties still need work.

117
00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:10.100
The authors suggest non-peptidic versions
and noncanonical amino acids next.

118
00:15:11.025 --> 00:15:18.325
And third, we still do not know exactly
when and where the natural cyclic imide
tags form in the body.

119
00:15:18.325 --> 00:15:24.775
David: One sentence to remember: the
degron is not just a tag cereblon reads.

120
00:15:25.625 --> 00:15:36.400
Rebuilt as a warhead, it gives you a
tunable handle on the ternary complex, and
the strength of that complex predicts how
well the degrader works.

121
00:15:37.475 --> 00:15:42.725
You have been listening to Protein
Degradation English Corner, from Litshare.

122
00:15:43.350 --> 00:15:44.300
See you next time.
